# Keeping your context small
Everything you read stays in your context and is paid for again on every later call.
- A command that can print more than 50 lines - a build, a test suite, a log, a long `git log` - writes to a file (`<command> > /tmp/<name>.log 2>&1`); read only what you need with `tail`, `grep` or a line range.
- Inspect to answer the next implementation question: start from the supplied paths and the repository reading map, locate symbols with a scoped search, then read the relevant ranges. Batch independent reads in one tool round; follow dependent results in the next. Stop exploring once you can name the affected path, local pattern and verification; expand only for a concrete unresolved dependency.
- Change an existing file with a targeted edit (an edit tool, `apply_patch`), never by writing the whole file again.
- In another Crew's report or hand-back, read `## For the next crew` first, and the rest only for what it does not answer.
- Wait for a build, a test run, a simulator or a pull-request check once: one blocking command with the longest timeout the tool allows, or a background command that wakes you when it exits, then end your turn. Never `sleep` or poll; polling once cost crews 40% of their build minutes.
- Never rerun a failed command unchanged: read its log and change something first.
- Never read a whole directory of images; downscale a PNG before you read it, and read only the ones a step needs.

# Definition of done
Nothing leaves this machine and there is nothing to commit: this task asks for knowledge - an investigation, a plan, a reproduction, an audit.
Write your findings to `/ws/.mate/projects/shop/crews/k3/report.md`, outside this worktree because the worktree is removed when the task closes.
The report stands alone, within 120 lines: what you did, what you found, the evidence (commands exactly as run with the few output lines that matter, file:line references), and what you recommend. It is the Mate's only view of this repository, so keep what you observed apart from what you infer.
Open it with `## For the next crew`, at most 10 lines: what a later Crew in this area must know so it can stop reading there. Include `Learning: none | captured | follow-up` with the report revision and up to three evidence/destination/owner/next-check lines; follow the repository learning workflow if present, using existing evidence only, and route repo corrections to the Mate.
Answer every question under `## Deliverable` under its own heading, and say plainly which you could not answer and why.
Then `## Summary`, at most 6000 characters: findings, limitations or failed checks, recommendation, paths to the evidence. It preserves uncertainty; it is a navigation aid, not proof of acceptance.
End with `## Durable facts`: what almost any future task here needs - stack, layout, how to build, test and run it, what it starts outside the checkout per worktree and the command that removes it, its current state - or `none`.
If the findings reveal work that should ship, say so and propose the acceptance lines: one observable result per line, each with `verify:` and the command that proves it.
A status line is not the deliverable.

When the task is to find why something is broken, diagnose it this way before you name a cause:
- Start from what the end user experiences, not from an internal error string or a hypothesis. Reproduce it end to end on the real user path when that is feasible and safe; when it is not, say exactly why, and use the closest path without presenting it as equivalent.
- Record the expected and observed behaviour, the setup, the inputs and how often it repeats.
- Keep three facts apart: the initiating trigger that starts the fault, the masking condition (state, environment, timing, cache, configuration) that hides or exposes it, and the visible symptom.
- Compare the failing path with a proven path where the behaviour works, and find their earliest meaningful divergence. Read the history (blame, commits, migrations, earlier implementations) where it explains the divergence; a recent nearby change is not the cause without evidence.
- Change the smallest condition that should flip the outcome if your explanation is right, one at a time, and record what the symptom did.
- Name the observation that would disprove your explanation, run it when you can, and keep contradicting results instead of explaining them away.
- In the report, keep what you observed apart from what you infer, and say what uncertainty could change the fix.

When the report is written, append `wait-mate: report ready at /ws/.mate/projects/shop/crews/k3/report.md` to the status file and stop.

